  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 8

Iteration 7 closed with a sentence that was a task: **every ratio in this file
is from a leaf.** A function containing both calls and control flow never
reaches the allocator that seven iterations have been improving. It goes to the
conservative path, where every value gets a stack slot whether or not anything
was short of registers.

Nobody had measured what that costs. This iteration measures it and does not
fix it.

## The isolation

Four fixtures, in two pairs. Each pair runs identical arithmetic through
identical calls and differs by one `if` whose taken arm is the same expression
— enough to change which path the function takes, and nothing else.

| live across calls | ISA | straight-line | with one `if` | |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 8 | AArch64 | 27.3 ns / 260 B | 28.8 ns / 392 B | +6% |
| 8 | x86-64 | 38.5 ns | 39.6 ns | +3% |
| **24** | **AArch64** | **62.2 ns / 828 B** | **82.5 ns / 1344 B** | **+33%** |
| **24** | **x86-64** | **95.3 ns** | **126.7 ns** | **+33%** |

At 24 values the conservative path takes **99 stack slots against 24** and
**325 instructions against 190**.

**The same factor on both ISAs.** That is the signature iteration 4 identified
for a cost living in the shared allocator rather than in either encoder, and it
is the third time this session that signature has pointed at the right place.

## Why this is not fixed here

The fix needs no new mechanism, which is exactly why it should not be done
quickly. Values live across a call have to survive caller-saved registers being
clobbered, and the preserved tier — added in iteration 6 — is a set of
registers that survives a call. What is missing is call-clobber handling in the
scan: at each call, move or spill the live values held in caller-saved
registers, preferring a preserved register over a slot.

That is the same corner of the same function where, earlier today, a hasty
version of proportional spilling produced a wrong answer that a green test
suite could not see. Calls, branches, phis and the frame policy all meet there.
The measurement is landed; the change is the next iteration's, with a benchmark
already in place to judge it.

## The part worth keeping: three rounds of agreeing, wrong numbers

Before these numbers there were three earlier rounds. All of them were
self-consistent, all of them plausible, all of them measuring the wrong
function.

`extract-native` reports an `:offset`. For every kernel measured in iterations
1 through 7 it was 0, because those modules export one function. These modules
define the helper first, so the exported kernel is at 72 — and the runner, told
0, executed the helper. It returned 337898, which is a real number: it is
`step(7)`.

Three consequences followed before it surfaced:

- **The 8-lane pair looked free** — 5.83 ns against 4.55 ns, the branch version
  apparently *faster*. Two identical runs of the same helper, differing by
  noise.
- I nearly wrote that down as a negative result: *the conservative path costs
  code size, not time.* It is a clean, publishable-sounding finding and it is
  false.
- Scaling to 24 lanes did not expose it. **The wrong measurement scaled just as
  cleanly as the right one would have**, because it was not measuring either
  workload.

What surfaced it was running the program through the reference interpreter and
finding 6210431302 where the artifact said 337898.

## Meta-review

- **Self-consistency is not a check.** Twenty-one rotated samples, low
  variance, both ISAs agreeing, a sensible scaling curve — every quality signal
  a measurement can carry, and all of them are properties of the measurement,
  not of the thing measured.
- **An oracle is the only check.** Iteration 7 said the guard that works is the
  one that runs the program. This is the same rule at the measurement layer:
  before believing a duration, check the answer.
- **The mistake was invisible in the direction I was looking.** I checked
  variance, rotation, load, and both ISAs. I did not check that the bytes I was
  timing were the function I named — and no amount of the first four would ever
  have shown it.
- **A negative result is exactly as dangerous as a positive one.** \"This costs
  nothing\" would have closed the question and removed the reason to look again.

## Next

Call-clobber handling in the scan, so that a function with calls and branches
stops taking the all-vreg path. The benchmark to judge it is landed, its answers
are known, and the offsets are written down."}]
